The target contributed zero native mask pixels at the same 88 of 160 placed-blocker positions in both engines. These positions spanned all 16 layouts. The wide panel hid the entire target at 76 of its 80 placed positions. The white box did so at 12 of 80. In MuJoCo, the reference, box and revisit matched exactly across opposite target states at all 88 positions and nowhere else. The native images agreed as well. In Isaac Sim, every one of the 320 retained/removed pairs differed at both resolutions, including the same 88 fully hidden positions. The other 160 positions per engine had the blocker parked. No parked-blocker pair matched in either engine.

The difference between renderers affects which decisions this test can certify. Semantic support remains unresolved on the unequal Isaac pairs. The calibration repeats below measure ordinary render variation at a fully hidden site.

The homography abstained on both members of all 88 MuJoCo pairs. Dense geometry did so on 87. At shelf stop P5 in layout PAM05, its registration passed, target similarity fell to 0.391, and obstruction scored 0.167. With image quality at 0.737, the frozen rule returned REMOVED for both worlds. This gave one correct removal and one false removal from the same evidence. Both commitments were unsupported. The zero-commitment criterion failed in MuJoCo and remained unassessed in Isaac.

The registered language model committed on four certified pairs across three layouts, including the pair in PAM05. All four received REMOVED twice. This produced eight unsupported definite decisions and four physical errors. Its geometry check had already stopped both members of the other 84 pairs. Across the complete panel the geometry check stopped 446 of 1,280 queries. The language model itself returned UNKNOWN on only two of the remaining 834.

A secondary comparison isolates the decision made after a successful registration. The dense rule and registered language model recorded the same map-validity fraction and admission decision on all 1,280 queries. Among the 419 admitted queries with the target removed, the dense rule found 399 removals and the language model found all 419. Among the 415 admitted queries with the target retained, the dense rule falsely reported removal twice and the language model did so 51 times. Of the 49 additional false removals, the dense rule had correctly answered PRESENT on 42 and abstained on seven. These counts describe the tested readers on a shared set of views.

